WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
Randomness appears in many different parts of C programs.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:15.960
It can drive simulations, randomized tests, sampling, identifiers, randomized algorithms, salts, nonces, tokens, keys, and correlation values.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:21.820
These uses may look similar at the call site, but they do not all require the same kind of randomness.

4
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.420
The required quality depends on the consequence of getting the value wrong.

5
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.560
A deterministic generator can be useful when a test needs repeatable behavior.

6
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.020
A simulation may need statistically reasonable distribution.

7
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.880
A security-sensitive token needs unpredictability.

8
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.980
A nonce may need uniqueness under a specific protocol rule.

9
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.160
A salt may need to avoid repetition without being secret.

10
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.680
One of the common mistakes is treating all random-looking output as equivalent.

11
00:00:51.780 --> 00:01:00.740
A generator that is acceptable for a local simulation may be completely inappropriate for a session identifier or key.

12
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.480
A value that is unique enough for logging may not be unpredictable enough for authorization.

13
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:13.920
C gives developers access to low-level APIs, platform calls, libraries, buffers, and integer conversions.

14
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.700
It does not automatically choose the right randomness source.

15
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:24.040
That choice belongs to the developer and should be visible in the design, documentation, and review.

16
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:33.680
Randomness mistakes can cause predictable behavior, collisions, weak identifiers, unreliable tests, operational surprises, or security defects.

17
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:45.220
A defensive approach begins by naming the purpose of the random value, choosing a source that matches that purpose, checking failures, and keeping test behavior separate from production behavior.
